  "result": "Perfect! All 301 tests pass. Here's a summary of the CSV export implementation:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've created a new **`src/export.js`** module with the `exportCsv(invoices)` function that exports invoices to CSV format.\n\n### Features\n\n**Header:** `number,customer,issueDate,dueDate,status,subtotal,discount,vat,total,due`\n\n**Format:**\n- Customer name (not ID) is looked up from the customer database\n- Amounts are in plain euros with 2 decimals and a dot: `1234.50`\n  - No currency sign (no \u20ac)\n  - No thousands separator (no commas in numbers)\n- Each line ends with `\\n`\n\n**CSV Escaping:**\n- Fields containing commas or double quotes are wrapped in double quotes\n- Double quotes within fields are doubled: `\"` becomes `\"\"`\n- Example: A customer name like `Marlow \"The Print Shop\"` becomes `\"Marlow \"\"The Print Shop\"\"\"`\n\n**Field Handling:**\n- Invoice number is empty string for draft invoices\n- Customer name is empty string if customer lookup fails\n- Due amount uses `balanceDue()` to account for payments and vouchers\n- All totals use proper rounding from `invoiceTotals()`\n\n### Tests\n\nAdded **31 comprehensive tests** covering:\n- Empty invoices\n- Single and multiple invoices\n- CSV escaping (commas, double quotes)\n- Amount formatting (plain euros, 2 decimals, no separators)\n- Complex customer names with special characters\n- Draft invoices (empty number)\n- Paid and partially paid invoices\n- Invoices with vouchers\n- Multiple payments\n- Various customer tiers\n- CSV field count validation\n- Invoice order preservation\n- Immutability of input\n\n**Total: 301 tests pass** (192 existing + 78 from reports + 31 from export)",
